<h2>Existentialism and Freedom</h2>

<p>Sartre’s existentialism makes two claims that pull against each other. The first: there are no objective moral norms; values are the products of human choice, not features of the world waiting to be discovered. The second: bad faith — the attempt to deny or evade one’s freedom — is wrong.</p>
<p>Both of these questions give you something genuinely to argue about. There is no predetermined correct answer. What matters is that you engage with Sartre’s actual text and reasoning, not just with your general feelings about freedom or morality.</p>

<div>
<p>Instructions</p>
<p>Choose <strong>one</strong> of the two questions below. Write an initial post of at least 200 words. Then respond substantively to at least <strong>two</strong> classmates who chose the same question — not just agreement or encouragement, but actual engagement with their argument. Responses should be at least 75 words.</p>
</div>

<div>
<p>Question 1 — Radical Freedom</p>
<p>Sartre claims that human beings are “condemned to be free” — that we cannot escape freedom, and that even refusing to choose is a form of choice. Do you find this convincing? Are there situations in which it seems clearly wrong — cases where people genuinely have no freedom at all? Or does Sartre’s distinction between <em>facticity</em> (the unchosen facts of your situation) and the freedom you exercise in responding to that situation successfully handle these cases?</p>

</div>

<div>
<p>Question 2 — Bad Faith and Moral Norms</p>
<p>Sartre says there are no objective moral norms, and also that bad faith is wrong. Is he entitled to both claims at once? Think about what resources he has within his own framework for condemning bad faith without appealing to external moral standards. Does his account of consciousness as a <em>for-itself</em> give him what he needs? Or is this an inconsistency that undermines his position?</p>
<p>You might also consider how this tension compares to one we saw in Unit 5: Marx’s difficulty in critiquing capitalism from within a framework that treats all moral positions as products of class interest. Is Sartre’s problem the same? Different?</p>
</div>

<div>
<p>What makes a strong post</p>
<p>•&nbsp;<strong>Engages with the text.</strong> Reference something specific from <em>Existentialism Is a Humanism</em> — a passage, an argument, an example. Do not rely on general impressions.</p>
<p>•&nbsp;<strong>Takes a position.</strong> Your post should have a point of view, not just a summary of what Sartre says. What do <em>you</em> think, and why?</p>
<p>•&nbsp;<strong>Responds to the argument, not the conclusion.</strong> If you disagree with Sartre, show where his argument fails, not just that you find his conclusion uncomfortable.</p>
<p>•&nbsp;<strong>Engages peers genuinely.</strong> In your replies, explain what in your classmate’s argument you are responding to. Agreement is fine; what is not fine is agreement without engagement.</p>
</div>
